1
00:00:00,325 --> 00:00:02,250
Did our ancestors kill every

2
00:00:02,250 --> 00:00:03,800
other kind of human?

3
00:00:04,225 --> 00:00:05,562
We do not have evidence for

4
00:00:05,562 --> 00:00:08,150
one coordinated global extermination

5
00:00:08,150 --> 00:00:09,225
campaign.

6
00:00:09,675 --> 00:00:12,625
As Homo sapiens expanded, competition

7
00:00:12,625 --> 00:00:14,850
for food, shelter, and territory

8
00:00:14,850 --> 00:00:16,950
could affect other populations.

9
00:00:16,950 --> 00:00:19,075
Violence is possible too.

10
00:00:19,075 --> 00:00:20,350
But different encounters

11
00:00:20,350 --> 00:00:22,100
could have different outcomes.

12
00:00:22,750 --> 00:00:24,075
Some groups may have avoided

13
00:00:24,075 --> 00:00:25,025
one another.

14
00:00:25,025 --> 00:00:27,100
Some may have exchanged knowledge.

15
00:00:27,100 --> 00:00:28,850
Genetic evidence shows that

16
00:00:28,850 --> 00:00:30,675
some had children together.

17
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:32,850
Environmental pressure and

18
00:00:32,850 --> 00:00:35,450
small, isolated populations

19
00:00:35,450 --> 00:00:36,938
could also make competition

20
00:00:36,938 --> 00:00:38,675
harder to withstand.

21
00:00:39,125 --> 00:00:40,475
That leaves a more difficult

22
00:00:40,475 --> 00:00:42,475
question: how did these factors

23
00:00:42,475 --> 00:00:44,400
interact in different places?

24
00:00:44,975 --> 00:00:46,375
It would be wrong to declare

25
00:00:46,375 --> 00:00:48,750
our ancestors uninvolved.

26
00:00:48,750 --> 00:00:50,425
It would also be wrong to turn

27
00:00:50,425 --> 00:00:51,800
every disappearance into the

28
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:54,025
same proven murder story.

29
00:00:54,450 --> 00:00:55,712
The evidence points toward

30
00:00:55,712 --> 00:00:58,125
a complicated history of contact,

31
00:00:58,125 --> 00:00:59,925
rather than one worldwide

32
00:00:59,925 --> 00:01:02,038
event with a single demonstrated

33
00:01:02,038 --> 00:01:03,000
cause.
